  "response": "The 2014\u20132016 northeast Pacific marine heatwave was nicknamed \"the Blob.\" The episode posed a classic attribution problem for physical oceanography. A large, coherent body of anomalously warm water appeared in the Gulf of Alaska and later spread along the North American coast, and researchers had to decide whether its origin lay chiefly in a transient atmospheric configuration or in the steady accumulation of heat in the upper ocean under rising greenhouse gas concentrations. Even the episode's popular label has its own record. The Blob was first named by Nick Bond in 2014; in other words, Bond, then Washington's state climatologist, coined the nickname that year in a newsletter describing the anomaly. Daniel Swain, by contrast, coined the nickname \"the Blob,\" a credit that circulated alongside Bond's in later commentary on the event.\n\nThe physical case began with the atmosphere. A persistent ridge of high atmospheric pressure sat over the northeast Pacific during the winter of 2013\u20132014, and because of it the transfer of heat from the ocean surface to the atmosphere was reduced. Under the ridge, the wintertime storm track was displaced northward, and the latent and sensible heat fluxes that normally cool the Gulf of Alaska each winter were markedly weaker than their climatological values. The establishment of that winter ridge preceded a period in which at least one of two oceanic processes, perhaps both, was operating. Weakened wind-driven mixing kept warm water concentrated near the ocean surface, or reduced advection of cold water into the region kept surface waters anomalously warm. Separating the contributions of these terms required quantitative accounting rather than inference from surface maps alone.\n\nThat accounting is where the competing explanations were tested. Although some researchers argued that long-term anthropogenic warming was a major contributor to the Blob's extreme temperatures, oceanographers used heat budget analyses of the upper ocean to attribute the warming to atmospheric forcing. A heat budget decomposes the change in mixed-layer temperature into air\u2013sea flux, horizontal advection, vertical entrainment, and residual terms, so that each mechanism's share can be estimated from observations such as Argo profiles and reanalysis fluxes. Bond and colleagues published their analysis of the 2014 warm anomaly in Nature. Their flux calculations, together with that publication, indicate the decisive result: Bond and colleagues concluded that the 2014 anomaly arose primarily from reduced ocean heat loss under the persistent atmospheric ridge. This conclusion settles the apparent tension between the anthropogenic argument and the budget results, because the budget showed that the anomalous flux term alone accounted for most of the observed temperature rise in the mixed layer, leaving little residual to be explained by a gradual background trend over a single season.\n\nAttribution studies, by contrast, compared climate model simulations with and without human greenhouse gas emissions. Such experiments ask a different question from a heat budget: not which physical terms produced the warming in a given year, but how much more probable a ridge of that persistence, or an ocean state of that warmth, becomes in a world altered by emissions. Their results bear on the likelihood of recurrence, whereas the budget analyses describe the mechanics of the event itself.\n\nThe magnitude of the event remains part of how its extremity is characterized. Observational products differ in baseline period, spatial averaging, and the depth at which temperature is sampled, and these choices determine whether the headline figure crosses a commonly cited threshold. Either the Blob's peak sea surface temperature anomaly exceeded 2\u00b0C above normal, or the Blob's peak sea surface temperature anomaly remained at or below 2\u00b0C above normal; the two characterizations cannot both be true, and one of them must hold for any single, consistently defined index. Whichever figure a given dataset supports, the mechanistic conclusion drawn from the upper-ocean heat budget does not depend on it, since that conclusion rests on the relative size of the flux terms rather than on the absolute peak anomaly.",
